WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.378
Logwatch groups production errors by their emitting source and files a ticket.

00:00:04.378 --> 00:00:12.067
For this walkthrough I staged a ticket around a real, locally reproduced defect: an invalid build timestamp crashes the version response.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:14.200
The ticket makes that origin explicit.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.358
Before handing it work, I ask for a dry run.

00:00:18.358 --> 00:00:23.668
The agent reads the repository and proposes a narrow fix with regression tests.

00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:26.357
The diff comes back as a ticket comment.

00:00:26.357 --> 00:00:29.920
Nothing is pushed, and the ticket stays where it was.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.800
Here is the actual proposal.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.620
It catches an invalid instant, keeps a warning, and adds tests at both ends of the numeric range.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.920
I can inspect that scope before approving the real run.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.093
Starting it here records my approval by adding the factory agent label.

00:00:47.093 --> 00:00:49.947
In Progress means the agent has claimed the ticket.

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:54.480
Linear remains the record; finishing an edit is only the beginning of the checks.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.260
The factory pushed these file edits and opened a pull request.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.435
The agent has no Bash tool and runs no build on the Pi.

00:01:02.435 --> 00:01:08.440
A push denylist protects secrets, deployment scripts, workflow configuration and the build tool wrapper.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.978
GitHub Actions builds and tests the exact commit, and the reviewer reads it.

00:01:13.978 --> 00:01:17.973
A rejection becomes the next repair brief, with three attempts in all.

00:01:17.973 --> 00:01:20.084
This diagram explains the retry path.

00:01:20.084 --> 00:01:23.680
The live checks determine whether this particular fix needs it.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.328
Temporal keeps the run and its waits durable.

00:01:27.328 --> 00:01:31.790
This is the real workflow for our staged ticket, rather than a progress animation.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:37.720
The agent has handed verification to GitHub, and a signal or the bounded polling wait brings back the result.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.844
The checks passed and the pull request merged.

00:01:41.844 --> 00:01:44.513
In Linear, Done follows that actual merge.

00:01:44.513 --> 00:01:52.840
The reviewer decides whether the changed paths permit auto merge; it does not rely on a model announcing that the work is finished.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.876
The repair handles bad metadata and keeps the warning.

00:01:56.876 --> 00:02:00.921
That distinction matters because a visitor can influence production log text.

00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:04.440
A change that silences something always needs a person to merge it.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.713
That was a reported defect becoming a checked fix and a completed ticket.

00:02:09.713 --> 00:02:14.120
The agent handles the small repair loop, while I choose which work it takes on.
